# Overview
#
# A negative contraction joins a verb and `not` into one word (`isn't`,
# `don't`). It hides the negation in a two-letter suffix, and a reader
# who skims past the `n't` reads the opposite claim. The fix writes the
# full form, so `not` gets a word of its own (`is not`, `do not`). The
# rule sits at Required, because it matches a fixed list of words and
# the fix is mechanical.
#
# Expression
#
# `main` lists the contractions. prose-lint straightens a curly
# apostrophe between two letters before Harper reads the text, so the
# list also matches a contraction with a curly apostrophe.
#
# Related Rules
#
# ProsePassive, ProseContrastiveNegation, ProseMinimizer,
# ProseDatedClaim, and ProseNotOnly keep their contracted forms, so `the
# value isn't written` draws a passive finding beside this one on the
# first pass. A contraction with a curly apostrophe draws this rule
# beside ProseTypography.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 1980 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 20 of 20 sampled
# hits were negative contractions in a sentence.
expr main [don't, doesn't, didn't, isn't, aren't, wasn't, weren't, can't, couldn't, won't, wouldn't, shouldn't, mustn't, hasn't, haven't, hadn't, needn't]
let message "Negative contraction. Write the full form, such as `does not` or `cannot`."
let description "Flags `don't`, `isn't`, `can't`, and the other negative contractions, which hide a negation in a suffix."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The cache does not expire."
allows "The client cannot retry."
